{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ltmhv2jgqj2cs3nqgxa3v3ri34y7vmqjixehqy5oh5h7gwpagp3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YFsDeJBuv2gUaUX1ftWW4d","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write G3 hashing differential tests","prompt":"You are writing Rust differential integration tests that compare a C libsodium 1.0.23 shared library against its Rust translation, through `libloading`, and FIXING the Rust translation wherever it diverges.\n\nROOT = /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg\n- `$ROOT/c_src/libsodium` — the C ground truth. READ ONLY. NEVER modify anything under c_src/.\n- `$ROOT/translation` — the Rust crate (cdylib `liblibsodium.so`). You MAY fix files under `$ROOT/translation/src/`.\n- `$ROOT/c_src/build/libsodium.so` — already built C library.\n\n## MANDATORY: how to build & run\nALWAYS use `$ROOT/translation/dt.sh` — never a bare `cargo test`. `cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib, so a bare run silently tests a stale library.\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation\n./dt.sh --test t03_g3_hash -- --test-threads=1        # run your file\n./dt.sh --test t03_g3_hash -- --exact some_test --nocapture\n```\nThere is NO network. Cargo must run offline (dt.sh already passes `--offline`).\nAlways redirect long output to a file and grep it, e.g. `./dt.sh --test t03_g3_hash > /tmp/o.log 2>&1; grep -E \"^test |panicked\" /tmp/o.log | head -50`. Use a writable temp dir: `$ROOT/_logs/` (writable) — do NOT use /tmp.\n\n## The existing harness — READ IT FIRST\n`$ROOT/translation/tests/common/mod.rs` provides everything. Read it, then reuse it. API:\n- `libs() -> &'static Libs` — `.c` and `.rs` are `&'static libloading::Library`, both already `sodium_init`ed.\n- `unsafe pair::<F>(\"symbol_name\") -> (Symbol<'static,F>, Symbol<'static,F>)` — the same symbol from C and Rust, typed as `F`. `.0` = C, `.1` = Rust.\n- `has_sym(name) -> bool`\n- `install_det_random()` — installs ONE deterministic `randombytes_implementation` into BOTH libraries so `*_keygen` / `*_random` become byte-comparable. Call it at the start of any test that uses randomness. It is idempotent.\n- `Rng::new(seed)` with `.next_u64()`, `.next_u32()`, `.byte()`, `.below(n)`, `.range(lo,hi)`, `.bytes(n)`, `.fill(&mut [u8])` — deterministic input generator.\n- `eq_bytes(ctx, c_out, rust_out)`, `eq_i32(ctx, c_ret, rust_ret)`, `hex(&[u8])`.\n- `errno()`, `set_errno(v)`, and constants `EINVAL EPERM ENOMEM ENOSYS ERANGE`. Both libraries share the calling thread's errno.\n- Abort-path comparison (for `sodium_misuse()` / `assert()` rows that kill the process):\n  `diff_abort_case(\"case_name\") -> Term { code, signal }` re-executes THIS test binary in two child processes (one per library) and asserts both died identically. To use it your test FILE must define\n  ```rust\n  #[test]\n  fn zz_abort_child() {\n      let Some((case, is_c)) = child_case() else { return };\n      let l = libs(); let h = if is_c { l.c } else { l.rs };\n      // ... dlsym from `h` and perform the dangerous call for `case` ...\n      std::process::exit(0); // reached only if it did NOT abort\n  }\n  ```\n  See `$ROOT/translation/tests/t02_g1_errors.rs` for a complete working example — COPY ITS STRUCTURE.\n\nStudy `$ROOT/translation/tests/t01_g1_utils.rs` (valid-path style) and `t02_g1_errors.rs` (error-path style) as the templates. Match their style: randomized inputs per configuration, one `#[test]` per coherent group of rows, context strings naming the row.\n\n## YOUR SCOPE — module group G3\nC modules: `crypto_generichash/` (blake2b), `crypto_shorthash/` (siphash24, siphashx24), `crypto_onetimeauth/` (poly1305), `crypto_auth/` (hmacsha256/512/512256), `crypto_kdf/` (blake2b, hkdf_sha256, hkdf_sha512), `crypto_hash/` (sha256, sha512, sha3), `crypto_xof/` (shake128/256, turboshake128/256), `crypto_core/keccak1600`.\n\nYour Phase A tables (already produced, DO NOT EDIT THEM):\n- `$ROOT/translation/CONFIGS.md` rows **281-509** are yours (valid-path configurations).\n- `$ROOT/translation/ERRORS.md` rows **282-404** are yours (rejection paths).\nRead those row ranges. Each row must end up covered by a test.\n\n## YOUR DELIVERABLES\nCreate exactly two files:\n1. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t03_g3_hash.rs` — Phase B, valid-path differential tests for CONFIGS.md rows 281-509.\n2. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t04_g3_hash_errors.rs` — Phase C, error-path differential tests for ERRORS.md rows 282-404 (plus generic boundaries: NULL pointers where C doesn't deref, zero and oversized lengths, one step past every documented range, and out-of-range enum/flag ints passed across FFI).\n\nRequirements:\n- EVERY test must call BOTH libraries via `pair::<F>(...)` (or dlsym from `libs().c` / `libs().rs`) and compare. NEVER call Rust code directly.\n- Exercise the LOW-LEVEL entry points directly (`crypto_generichash_blake2b_init_salt_personal`, `crypto_generichash_blake2b_update`, `_final`, `crypto_hash_sha512_init/update/final`, `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha256_extract_init/extract_update/extract_final/expand`, `crypto_xof_*_init/absorb/squeeze`, `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_init/update/final`, `crypto_auth_hmacsha512_init/update/final`, ...), not just one-shot wrappers.\n- Use MANY randomized inputs per configuration with a FIXED seed. Cover chunk-split shapes (1 byte at a time, at rate/block boundaries 63/64/65, 127/128/129, 135/136/137, 71/72/73, 167/168/169), empty input, keyed and unkeyed, every valid output length, key lengths that cross the HMAC block-hashing threshold (>64 / >128).\n- State structs: get their size from the exported `*_statebytes()` function and allocate `vec![0u8; n]` (aligned via a `Vec<u64>` when you need alignment). Compare the OUTPUT bytes; you may also compare state bytes when the C struct has no padding, but if state-byte comparison produces spurious mismatches from uninitialised padding, compare only the outputs and say so in a comment.\n- Add a comment on each test naming the CONFIGS.md / ERRORS.md row numbers it covers.\n\n## WHEN A TEST FAILS\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix the Rust in `$ROOT/translation/src/`. Relevant Rust files: `blake2b.rs`, `hash_sha256.rs`, `hash_sha512.rs`, `hash_sha3.rs`, `xof.rs`, `keccak1600.rs`, `auth_hmac.rs`, `poly1305.rs`, `shorthash.rs`, `kdf.rs`, `crypto_hash.rs`. Read the corresponding C file, find the exact discrepancy, fix the Rust, rerun. Never weaken a test to make it pass, and never \"fix\" the C.\nIf a test of yours turns out to encode a WRONG expectation (e.g. you assumed a bound is enforced when the C does not enforce it), fix the TEST to assert C/Rust agreement instead of your assumption — that is the correct resolution, and note it in a comment.\n\n## FINISH CRITERIA\n`./dt.sh --test t03_g3_hash -- --test-threads=1` and `./dt.sh --test t04_g3_hash_errors -- --test-threads=1` both report `test result: ok` with 0 failures, and between them they cover all of CONFIGS.md 281-509 and ERRORS.md 282-404.\n\nReport back: the two file paths, the number of `#[test]` functions in each, the final `test result:` lines, the list of CONFIGS/ERRORS row numbers you could NOT cover (with the reason), and every change you made under translation/src/ (file + what was wrong + the fix).","subagent_type":"general-purpose"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":744,"cache_read_input_tokens":152735,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":744,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f","uuid":"1b83130f-5935-4cb0-904d-624e89f3f568","timestamp":"2026-09-08T15:23:48.614Z"}
