{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cmuxbywbfdnowdf42fornowxipyh7b44qvcbl7n6fawxhimalmra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01QiaBM6Z2JFrEYVe6tn7un9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write G5 asymmetric differential tests","prompt":"You are writing Rust differential integration tests that compare a C libsodium 1.0.23 shared library against its Rust translation, through `libloading`, and FIXING the Rust translation wherever it diverges.\n\nROOT = /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg\n- `$ROOT/c_src/libsodium` — the C ground truth. READ ONLY. NEVER modify anything under c_src/.\n- `$ROOT/translation` — the Rust crate (cdylib `liblibsodium.so`). You MAY fix files under `$ROOT/translation/src/`.\n- `$ROOT/c_src/build/libsodium.so` — already built C library.\n\n## IMPORTANT: other agents are working in this same crate concurrently\nOther agents are creating OTHER test files and may be editing OTHER files under `translation/src/`. Therefore:\n- ONLY create/edit the two test files assigned to you below. Never touch another `tests/*.rs`.\n- Only edit `src/` files in YOUR list (below). If you believe a fix belongs in a file outside your list, report it instead of editing it.\n- If a build fails with an error in a file you never touched, another agent is mid-edit: wait ~30s and retry the build.\n\n## MANDATORY: how to build & run\nALWAYS use `$ROOT/translation/dt.sh` — never a bare `cargo test` (it does NOT rebuild the cdylib, so you'd silently test a stale library).\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation\n./dt.sh --test t07_g5_asym -- --test-threads=1\n./dt.sh --test t07_g5_asym -- --exact some_test --nocapture\n```\nNo network; cargo runs offline (dt.sh passes `--offline`). Redirect long output to a file under `$ROOT/_logs/` (writable) and grep it. Do NOT use /tmp.\n\n## The existing harness — READ IT FIRST\n`$ROOT/translation/tests/common/mod.rs`. API:\n- `libs() -> &'static Libs` — `.c` / `.rs` are `&'static libloading::Library`, both already `sodium_init`ed.\n- `unsafe pair::<F>(\"symbol\") -> (Symbol<'static,F>, Symbol<'static,F>)` — `.0` = C, `.1` = Rust.\n- `has_sym(name) -> bool`\n- `install_det_random()` — installs ONE deterministic `randombytes_implementation` into BOTH libraries so `*_keypair`, `*_keygen`, `crypto_core_*_random`, `crypto_box_seal` become byte-comparable. Idempotent; call at the start of any test that uses randomness. Note: this makes the NON-deterministic entry points fully comparable, so test them too.\n- `Rng::new(seed)` with `.next_u64() .next_u32() .byte() .below(n) .range(lo,hi) .bytes(n) .fill(&mut [u8])`.\n- `eq_bytes(ctx, c_out, rust_out)`, `eq_i32(ctx, c_ret, rust_ret)`, `hex(&[u8])`.\n- `errno()`, `set_errno(v)`, constants `EINVAL EPERM ENOMEM ENOSYS ERANGE`.\n- Abort-path comparison for `sodium_misuse()`/`assert()` rows: `diff_abort_case(\"case\") -> Term { code, signal }` re-executes THIS binary in two children (one per library) and asserts both died identically. Your test FILE must define:\n  ```rust\n  #[test]\n  fn zz_abort_child() {\n      let Some((case, is_c)) = child_case() else { return };\n      let l = libs(); let h = if is_c { l.c } else { l.rs };\n      // dlsym from `h`, perform the dangerous call for `case`\n      std::process::exit(0); // reached only if it did NOT abort\n  }\n  ```\n  `$ROOT/translation/tests/t02_g1_errors.rs` is a complete working example — COPY ITS STRUCTURE.\n\nStudy `$ROOT/translation/tests/t01_g1_utils.rs` (valid-path style) and `t02_g1_errors.rs` (error-path style) as templates.\n\n## YOUR SCOPE — module group G5\nC modules: `crypto_box/` (curve25519xsalsa20poly1305, curve25519xchacha20poly1305, easy, seal), `crypto_kx/`, `crypto_scalarmult/` (curve25519 ref10, ed25519, ristretto255), `crypto_sign/` (ed25519 ref10), `crypto_core/ed25519` and ristretto255.\n\nYour Phase A tables (already produced, DO NOT EDIT THEM):\n- `$ROOT/translation/CONFIGS.md` rows **717-888** are yours.\n- `$ROOT/translation/ERRORS.md` rows **509-638** are yours.\nRead those row ranges. Each row must end up covered by a test.\n\nFacts already established about this build:\n- Neither `ED25519_NONDETERMINISTIC` nor `ED25519_COMPAT` is defined, so signing is fully deterministic and byte-comparable. The live signature check is `(sig[63] & 240) != 0 && sc25519_is_canonical(sig+32) == 0`; the `sig[63] & 224` branch is dead code.\n- `crypto_core_ed25519_from_uniform` / `from_hash` DO NOT EXIST in 1.0.23. The map-to-curve API is `crypto_core_ed25519_from_string{,_nu}(ctx, msg, hash_alg)`. `crypto_core_ristretto255_from_hash` DOES exist. Verify with `nm -D` before writing a test for a symbol.\n- `crypto_core_ed25519_add/sub` do only `frombytes` + `is_on_curve` — they ACCEPT non-canonical encodings, the identity, and small-order points, unlike `is_valid_point`. Reproduce, do not \"fix\".\n- `crypto_sign_ed25519_pk_to_curve25519` omits the `ge25519_is_canonical` check that `verify_detached` performs.\n- `crypto_core_ed25519_scalar_invert` only tests literal byte-zero, so `s = L` returns 0 with a meaningless `recip`.\n- `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519(n=0)` still WRITES `q` before returning -1.\n- `crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305` has NO NaCl zero-padded API; only xsalsa20 has `crypto_box()`/`crypto_box_open()` (which reject `mlen < 32` / `clen < 32`).\n- `crypto_box_seal` writes the ephemeral pk into `c[0..31]` even when it returns -1.\n- `crypto_kx_{client,server}_session_keys` reach `sodium_misuse()` only when BOTH `rx` and `tx` are NULL; a single NULL is a valid configuration.\n\n## YOUR DELIVERABLES\nCreate exactly two files:\n1. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t07_g5_asym.rs` — Phase B valid-path tests for CONFIGS.md rows 717-888.\n2. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t08_g5_asym_errors.rs` — Phase C error-path tests for ERRORS.md rows 509-638, plus generic boundaries (NULL where C doesn't deref, zero/oversized lengths, one step past every range, out-of-range `hash_alg`/enum ints across FFI).\n\nRequirements:\n- EVERY test calls BOTH libraries via `pair::<F>(...)` and compares. NEVER call Rust code directly.\n- Exercise the LOW-LEVEL entry points: `crypto_box_beforenm`/`_afternm`/`_open_afternm` and their easy/detached afternm forms, the NaCl zero-padded `crypto_box`/`crypto_box_open`, `_seal`/`_seal_open`, `_keypair`/`_seed_keypair`; `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519`/`_base`, `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519`/`_base`/`_noclamp`/`_base_noclamp`, `crypto_scalarmult_ristretto255`/`_base`; `crypto_sign_ed25519_detached`/`_verify_detached` AND the streaming `crypto_sign_ed25519ph_init/update/final_create/final_verify` with multi-chunk updates; `crypto_sign_ed25519_sk_to_seed`/`_sk_to_pk`/`_pk_to_curve25519`/`_sk_to_curve25519`; the whole `crypto_core_ed25519_*` and `crypto_core_ristretto255_*` scalar arithmetic set (`scalar_add/sub/mul/negate/complement/invert/reduce/random`, `add/sub/is_valid_point/random/from_string/from_hash`).\n- MANY randomized inputs per configuration with a FIXED seed. For the scalar/field arithmetic use thousands of random values PLUS the structured edge values: 0, 1, 2, L-1, L, L+1, 2^252+..., all-0xff, and 64-byte non-reduced inputs including all-0xff.\n- For point inputs, include the concrete known-bad encodings: all-zero, the canonical curve25519 small-order points, non-canonical field elements (`...ff 7f`, `ec ff..ff 7f`), y >= p, and random 32-byte strings (most of which are not valid points) — the -1/0 result must match.\n- Message lengths for sign/box: 0,1,31,32,33,64,127,128,1000.\n- Add a comment on each test naming the CONFIGS.md / ERRORS.md row numbers it covers.\n\n## WHEN A TEST FAILS\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix the Rust. YOUR editable src files: `box_.rs`, `kx.rs`, `scalarmult.rs`, `sign_ed25519.rs`, `x25519_ref10.rs`, `core_ed25519.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_fe.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_ge.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_ristretto.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_sc.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_sc_mul.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_sc_muladd.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_sc_reduce.rs`, `ed25519_ref10_tables.rs`. Read the corresponding C, find the exact discrepancy, fix the Rust, rerun. Never weaken a test to make it pass; never \"fix\" the C.\nIf a test of yours encodes a WRONG expectation (you assumed a check the C does not perform), change the TEST to assert C/Rust agreement, and note it in a comment.\n\n## FINISH CRITERIA\n`./dt.sh --test t07_g5_asym -- --test-threads=1` and `./dt.sh --test t08_g5_asym_errors -- --test-threads=1` both report `test result: ok` with 0 failures, covering CONFIGS.md 717-888 and ERRORS.md 509-638.\n\nReport back: the two file paths, the number of `#[test]` functions in each, the final `test result:` lines, any rows you could NOT cover (with reasons), and every change you made under translation/src/ (file + what was wrong + the fix).","subagent_type":"general-purpose"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8248,"cache_read_input_tokens":153479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8248,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f","uuid":"6953e2c0-deda-4aa6-8c67-e98072ba5a8f","timestamp":"2026-09-08T15:25:12.067Z"}
