<F>(\"symbol\") -> (Symbol<'static,F>, Symbol<'static,F>)` — `.0` = C, `.1` = Rust.\n- `has_sym(name) -> bool`\n- `install_det_random()` — installs ONE deterministic `randombytes_implementation` (defined in the harness as `DET_IMPL`, with `.uniform = NULL` so the library's own rejection-sampling loop runs) into BOTH libraries, making `randombytes_buf` and every consumer byte-comparable. Idempotent. `det_reseed(seed)` resets the shared stream — call it before each paired C/Rust call when you need the two libraries to draw the SAME bytes (draw for C, `det_reseed` back, then draw for Rust).\n- `Rng::new(seed)` with `.next_u64() .next_u32() .byte() .below(n) .range(lo,hi) .bytes(n) .fill(&mut [u8])`.\n- `eq_bytes(ctx, c_out, rust_out)`, `eq_i32(ctx, c_ret, rust_ret)`, `hex(&[u8])`.\n- `errno()`, `set_errno(v)`, constants `EINVAL EPERM ENOMEM ENOSYS ERANGE`.\n- `RandombytesImpl` — the `repr(C)` `randombytes_implementation` vtable, so you can build your own custom implementations and pass them to `randombytes_set_implementation`.\n- Abort-path comparison: `diff_abort_case(\"case\") -> Term { code, signal }` re-executes THIS binary in two children (one per library) and asserts both died identically. Your test FILE must define:\n  ```rust\n  #[test]\n  fn zz_abort_child() {\n      let Some((case, is_c)) = child_case() else { return };\n      let l = libs(); let h = if is_c { l.c } else { l.rs };\n      // dlsym from `h`, perform the dangerous call for `case`\n      std::process::exit(0); // reached only if it did NOT abort\n  }\n  ```\n  `$ROOT/translation/tests/t02_g1_errors.rs` is a complete working example — COPY ITS STRUCTURE.\n\nStudy `$ROOT/translation/tests/t01_g1_utils.rs` and `t02_g1_errors.rs` as templates.\n\n## YOUR SCOPE — module group G6\nC modules: `randombytes/` (randombytes.c, internal/randombytes_internal_random.c, sysrandom/randombytes_sysrandom.c), `crypto_kem/` (mlkem768 ref, xwing), `crypto_ipcrypt/` (crypto_ipcrypt.c, ipcrypt_soft.c).\n\nYour Phase A tables (already produced, DO NOT EDIT THEM):\n- `$ROOT/translation/CONFIGS.md` rows **889-995** are yours.\n- `$ROOT/translation/ERRORS.md` rows **639-739** are yours.\nRead those row ranges. Each row must end up covered by a test.\n\nFacts already established:\n- There is NO IP-string API. `crypto_ipcrypt.h` exposes only the 16-byte binary API. Do not write tests for `str_to_ip16`-style functions.\n- `crypto_ipcrypt` has zero error paths; every entry point returns `void`, no length checks. There IS an undocumented **pfx** variant (`crypto_ipcrypt_pfx_keygen/_pfx_encrypt/_pfx_decrypt`, PFX_KEYBYTES=32), plus a data-dependent degenerate-key branch in the ndx/pfx variants (when the two key halves yield equal middle round keys, k2 is re-derived from `k[i] ^ 0x5a`). That branch MUST be exercised — construct keys that hit it.\n- Naming: libsodium uses `*_seed_keypair` and `*_enc_deterministic` (NOT `_keypair_derand`/`_enc_derand`), and `randombytes_stir`/`randombytes_close`. Seed sizes: mlkem768 `seed_keypair`=64 B, `enc_deterministic`=32 B; xwing `seed_keypair`=32 B, `enc_deterministic`=64 B. VERIFY every symbol with `nm -D $ROOT/c_src/build/libsodium.so | grep ...` before writing a test for it.\n- Only real KEM `-1` paths: `crypto_kem_mlkem768_ref_enc_deterministic` rejects a non-canonical pk (any 12-bit coefficient >= 3329 — reachable with `pk[0]=pk[1]=0xff`); `crypto_kem_xwing_enc`/`_dec` return -1 when X25519 yields an all-zero shared secret (low-order point in `pk[1184..1216]` / `ct[1088..1120]`).\n- `crypto_kem_mlkem768_ref_dec` NEVER returns -1: implicit rejection via `fail = sodium_memcmp(ct, cmp, 1088); fail_mask = (unsigned)fail >> 31; cmov` swapping in `k_bar = SHAKE256(z ‖ ct)[0..32]` with `z = sk[2368..2400]`. There is no hpk consistency check. So decapsulating a CORRUPTED ciphertext must produce a byte-IDENTICAL pseudorandom shared secret in C and Rust — assert byte equality, not \"both failed\".\n- `randombytes_set_implementation` never validates (returns 0 even for NULL). Both shipped implementations set `.uniform = NULL`, so `randombytes_uniform`'s default rejection loop always runs. `randombytes_uniform(0)` and `(1)` return 0 without consuming randomness. `randombytes_buf_deterministic` aborts via `sodium_misuse()` only for size > 274877906944.\n\n## YOUR DELIVERABLES\nCreate exactly two files:\n1. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t09_g6_rand.rs` — Phase B valid-path tests for CONFIGS.md rows 889-995.\n2. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t10_g6_rand_errors.rs` — Phase C error-path tests for ERRORS.md rows 639-739, plus generic boundaries (NULL where C doesn't deref, zero/oversized lengths, one step past every documented range, out-of-range ints across FFI).\n\nRequirements:\n- EVERY test calls BOTH libraries via `pair::<F>(...)` and compares. NEVER call Rust code directly.\n- randombytes: `randombytes_buf_deterministic` over many seeds x sizes 0/1/31/32/33/63/64/65/1000/4096 (byte equality); `randombytes_seedbytes`; `randombytes_implementation_name` for both shipped implementations, switched via `randombytes_set_implementation(&randombytes_internal_implementation)` and `(&randombytes_sysrandom_implementation)` (these are exported DATA symbols — dlsym them and pass the address); `randombytes_uniform` with a CUSTOM deterministic implementation installed into both libraries so the rejection-sampling loop is byte-comparable, over upper_bound 0,1,2,3,255,256,257,0x7fffffff,0x80000000,0xffffffff,0xfffffffe; `randombytes_random`; `randombytes_stir`/`randombytes_close` idempotency and return values; the NaCl `randombytes(buf, len)` entry point; `randombytes_internal_random_*` entry points.\n- crypto_kem: both mlkem768 and xwing; the deterministic `*_seed_keypair` and `*_enc_deterministic` over many fixed seeds (byte equality is the highest-value assertion here); `*_keypair`+`enc`+`dec` round trip under `install_det_random()`; decapsulation of a valid ciphertext, and of ciphertexts corrupted at many different byte positions (assert byte-identical implicit-rejection secret); every byte-length and primitive-name getter; the generic `crypto_kem_*` dispatch layer.\n- crypto_ipcrypt: deterministic variant encrypt/decrypt; the nd and ndx variants with EXPLICIT tweaks; the pfx variant; IPv4-mapped (`00..00 ff ff a b c d`) vs native IPv6 16-byte inputs; all-zero and all-0xff inputs; round-trips; and keys that trigger the degenerate-key re-derivation branch.\n- MANY randomized inputs per configuration, FIXED seed.\n- Add a comment on each test naming the CONFIGS.md / ERRORS.md row numbers it covers.\n\n## WHEN A TEST FAILS\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix the Rust. YOUR editable src files: `randombytes.rs`, `randombytes_internal.rs`, `randombytes_sysrandom.rs`, `kem.rs`, `kem_mlkem768_ref.rs`, `ipcrypt.rs`. (`softaes.rs`, `keccak1600.rs`, `xof.rs` are owned by other agents — if the bug is there, report it instead of editing.) Read the corresponding C, find the exact discrepancy, fix the Rust, rerun. Never weaken a test to make it pass; never \"fix\" the C.\nIf a test of yours encodes a WRONG expectation, change the TEST to assert C/Rust agreement, and note it in a comment.\n\n## FINISH CRITERIA\n`./dt.sh --test t09_g6_rand -- --test-threads=1` and `./dt.sh --test t10_g6_rand_errors -- --test-threads=1` both report `test result: ok` with 0 failures, covering CONFIGS.md 889-995 and ERRORS.md 639-739.\n\nReport back: the two file paths, the number of `#[test]` functions in each, the final `test result:` lines, any rows you could NOT cover (with reasons), and every change you made under translation/src/ (file + what was wrong + the fix).","uuid":"816faa74-7944-47be-86ee-471c91a38abd","session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f"}
