c`, `pbkdf2-sha256.c`, `nosse/pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256_nosse.c`), and `crypto_pwhash.c`.\n\nYour Phase A tables (already produced, DO NOT EDIT THEM):\n- `$ROOT/translation/CONFIGS.md` rows **164-280** are yours.\n- `$ROOT/translation/ERRORS.md` rows **100-281** are yours.\nRead those row ranges. Each row must end up covered by a test.\n\nFacts already established:\n- Argon2 internal enums are fully erased at the public boundary: every `ARGON2_*` code collapses to `return -1`. ONLY `ARGON2_VERIFY_MISMATCH` additionally sets `errno = EINVAL`; all other decode/validate failures leave errno untouched. Assert errno too.\n- Two abort paths: `crypto_pwhash_str_alg` with `alg ∉ {1,2}` calls `sodium_misuse()`; `escrypt_PBKDF2_SHA256` with `dkLen > 0x1fffffffe0` calls `sodium_misuse()`.\n- `OPSLIMIT_MIN` is asymmetric: argon2i = 3, argon2id = 1. So `crypto_pwhash(alg=1, opslimit=1)` fails while `(alg=2, opslimit=1)` succeeds.\n- `memlimit` is truncated by `/1024U` before becoming `m_cost`, so memlimit 8192 and 8193 must produce IDENTICAL digests, and `str_needs_rehash` returns 0 for memlimit differences under 1024. High-value differential case — test it.\n- scrypt one-shot does NOT range-check opslimit/memlimit: `pickparams` silently clamps opslimit up to 32768 and accepts memlimit=0 (N=2). `crypto_pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256(..., opslimit=0, memlimit=0)` SUCCEEDS. `scrypt_..._str_verify` skips passwdlen checks and requires `strlen(str) == 101` exactly.\n- Encoding: `$v=` is mandatory and must be exactly 19; `v=16` → `ARGON2_INCORRECT_TYPE`, not DECODING_FAIL. Base64 is `ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING`, so an `=` causes DECODING_FAIL. Leading zeros in m/t/p/v are rejected. An `$argon2id$` string given to the argon2i decoder yields DECODING_FAIL.\n- `lanes > 1` is unreachable via the public hashing APIs (parallelism is hardcoded to 1); `p=2/4` is only reachable through `argon2_decode_string` in `*_str_verify` / `*_str_needs_rehash`. That is the only route to `ARGON2_MEMORY_TOO_LITTLE` from `m_cost < 8*lanes`.\n- Dead branches you should NOT write tests for: all `PASSWD_MIN` checks (MIN=0), `ARGON2_PWD_TOO_SHORT`, `AD_*`, `SECRET_*`, `THREADS_TOO_*`, all `pickparams() != 0` paths, and the post-`DECIMAL_U32` `> UINT32_MAX` re-checks.\n- Cheapest params that still exercise the real paths: argon2id `opslimit=1, memlimit=8192`; argon2i `opslimit=3, memlimit=8192`; scrypt `_ll` with `N=2, r=1, p=1`. Do NOT use INTERACTIVE/MODERATE/SENSITIVE profiles except for ONE spot-check each of INTERACTIVE, and never MODERATE/SENSITIVE.\n- `$ROOT/_logs/frag/G2_configs.md` ends with an HTML comment listing the too-slow combinations and the cheapest substitutes — read it.\n\n## YOUR DELIVERABLES\nCreate exactly two files:\n1. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t11_g2_pwhash.rs` — Phase B valid-path tests for CONFIGS.md rows 164-280.\n2. `$ROOT/translation/tests/t12_g2_pwhash_errors.rs` — Phase C error-path tests for ERRORS.md rows 100-281, plus generic boundaries (zero/oversized lengths, one step past every documented limit, and out-of-range `alg` ints across the FFI boundary — C enums accept any int, so `alg = 0, 3, -1, 255, INT_MAX` are real inputs).\n\nRequirements:\n- EVERY test calls BOTH libraries via `pair::<F>(...)` and compares outputs byte-for-byte, return values, AND errno. NEVER call Rust code directly.\n- Cover the FULL entry-point set: `crypto_pwhash`, `crypto_pwhash_str`, `crypto_pwhash_str_alg`, `crypto_pwhash_str_verify`, `crypto_pwhash_str_needs_rehash`; `crypto_pwhash_argon2i_*` and `crypto_pwhash_argon2id_*` (the same five each); `crypto_pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256`, `_str`, `_str_verify`, `_str_needs_rehash`, and the low-level `crypto_pwhash_scryptsalsa208sha256_ll(passwd, passwdlen, salt, saltlen, N, r, p, buf, buflen)`; plus every constant/getter (`*_bytes_min/max`, `*_saltbytes`, `*_strbytes`, `*_strprefix`, `*_opslimit_*`, `*_memlimit_*`, `*_alg_*`, `*_primitive`).\n- `_ll` is fast and fully deterministic — hammer it with MANY randomized `(N, r, p, buflen)` combinations (N a power of two: 2,4,8,16,1024; r 1..8; p 1..4; buflen 1,16,32,64,127,128) plus the RFC 7914 vectors 1-3.\n- For the argon2/scrypt one-shots, use a FIXED seed and randomized passwords/salts, but keep the parameters cheap. Vary output length across BYTES_MIN(16), 17, 32, 64, 100.\n- For `*_str_verify` / `*_str_needs_rehash`, feed a large table of hand-built encoded strings covering every decoder branch (valid, wrong prefix, missing/extra `$`, `v=16`, `v=20`, leading zeros, base64 with `=`, salt too short, p=2, p=4, m too small for the lane count, truncated, over-long, embedded NUL, exactly-101-byte scrypt strings and 100/102-byte ones).\n- Add a comment on each test naming the CONFIGS.md / ERRORS.md row numbers it covers.\n\n## WHEN A TEST FAILS\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix the Rust. YOUR editable src files: `pwhash_argon2.rs`, `argon2.rs`, `argon2_encoding.rs`, `pwhash_scrypt.rs`, `scrypt.rs`. (`blake2b.rs`, `hash_sha256.rs` are owned by another agent — if the bug is there, report it instead of editing.) Read the corresponding C, find the exact discrepancy, fix the Rust, rerun. Never weaken a test to make it pass; never \"fix\" the C.\nIf a test of yours encodes a WRONG expectation, change the TEST to assert C/Rust agreement, and note it in a comment.\n\n## FINISH CRITERIA\n`./dt.sh --test t11_g2_pwhash -- --test-threads=1` and `./dt.sh --test t12_g2_pwhash_errors -- --test-threads=1` both report `test result: ok` with 0 failures, covering CONFIGS.md 164-280 and ERRORS.md 100-281, and each finishing well under 600 s.\n\nReport back: the two file paths, the number of `#[test]` functions in each, the final `test result:` lines, any rows you could NOT cover (with reasons), and every change you made under translation/src/ (file + what was wrong + the fix).","uuid":"ba399131-1da6-429f-b16c-82fbe1cce5e1","session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f"}
