renced by comment and exercised.\n\n## Rows where the Phase-A table's stated expectation was WRONG (test changed to assert C/Rust agreement, noted in a comment)\n- **CONFIGS 766** — pairs the RFC 7748 §5.2 *input scalar* `a546e36b…` with the §6.1 *Alice public key* `8520f009…`; they don't belong together. Replaced with the real §6.1 Alice **and** Bob vectors (both verified against C); `a546e36b…` is still exercised.\n- **CONFIGS 778** — claims `base_noclamp(8) == base(1)`. False: `_crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_clamp` does `k[0] &= 248; k[31] |= 64`, so `base(1)` multiplies by 2^254. Replaced with the real invariant `base(n) == base_noclamp(clamp(n))`, asserted for `n = 1` and 250 random `n`.\n- **CONFIGS 870** — the literal `3066f82a…` is not what 1.0.23 produces for `from_hash(SHA512(\"Ristretto is traditionally a small shot of espresso coffee\"))` (the draft tabulates the 64-byte map input, not a pre-image). Reduced to C/Rust agreement + `is_valid_point == 1`.\n- **CONFIGS 838/839/875/876** — `scalar_add(s, negate(s)) == 0` / `+ complement(s) == 1` hold only for **canonical** inputs: `crypto_core_ed25519_scalar_add` calls `sodium_add(x_, y_, 32)`, discarding the carry out of bit 255. The identities are now asserted for canonical inputs; non-canonical inputs (e.g. all-`0xff`) still flow through every entry point with C/Rust agreement asserted.\n- **ERRORS 586** — my first draft asserted `sig[63] & 240 == 0` for a `sig[63] & 224` probe; impossible since `0xe0 ⊂ 0xf0`. Rewritten with the correct reasoning (any `S` with a bit of `0xe0` set is ≥ 2^253 > L, so never canonical ⇒ the `ED25519_COMPAT` branch is behaviourally indistinguishable from the live check) and all four high bit patterns probed.\n\n## Rows not separately reachable (documented in-test, not skipped silently)\n- **ERRORS 517/518** — `d |= q[i]` all-zero ladder output and `implementation->mult() != 0`; both `LCOV_EXCL` and unreachable from the public API (every small-order `p` is caught by `has_small_order` first; ref10 `mult` always returns 0). A 600-case random sweep asserts return-value agreement regardless of which branch fires.\n- **ERRORS 589** — `smlen - 64 > SODIUM_SIZE_MAX - 64` requires `smlen > SIZE_MAX`, unrepresentable in `unsigned long long` on this target.\n- **ERRORS 591** — `crypto_sign_ed25519_detached` has no failure path, so the combined-sign error branch is unreachable; asserted 0 for 63 arbitrary `sk` values incl. all-zero and all-`0xff`.\n- **ERRORS 616/617/618** — `_string_to_points(n > 2)`, `assert(h_len <= 0xff)`, `ge25519_xmont_to_ymont` failure: all `LCOV_EXCL` `abort()`/`assert()` sites with no public entry point. The three sizes that feed row 617 are pinned instead.\n- **ERRORS 638** — confirmed by `has_sym` that `crypto_core_ed25519_from_uniform`/`_from_hash` are absent and that `_from_string{,_nu}` / `crypto_core_ristretto255_from_hash` are present.\n\n## Confirmed-as-designed C quirks reproduced (not \"fixed\")\n`crypto_core_ed25519_add/sub` accept non-canonical, identity and small-order encodings (609); `scalar_invert` returns 0 for `s = L`/`2L` (613/635); `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519{,_base}(n=0)` clobbers `q` before returning −1 (524/527); `crypto_box_seal` writes `c[0..32]` even on −1 and leaves `c[32..]` untouched (566); `pk_to_curve25519` omits the `ge25519_is_canonical` check that `verify_detached` performs (595); a single NULL `rx`/`tx` in `crypto_kx_*_session_keys` is valid, only both-NULL aborts (574/575); `crypto_box` accepts a non-zero-padded message (563).","usage":{"total_tokens":273926,"tool_uses":48,"duration_ms":1833762},"uuid":"bc71c01a-4d91-4dc9-8280-6d6ec0d6122a","session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_wjvs5pvbf6dwwxz7j4sc4gmieapy6apo3vem5tqzgtqxegh3a3fa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both deliverables are complete and green.\n\n## Files\n\n| File | `#[test]` fns | Final result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation/tests/t09_g6_rand.rs` (Phase B, CONFIGS 889-995) | **41** | `test result: ok. 41 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.40s` |\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation/tests/t10_g6_rand_errors.rs` (Phase C, ERRORS 639-739) | **25** | `test result: ok. 25 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 16.08s` |\n\nEvery test calls both libraries via `pair::<F>(...)` (or per-library `dlsym` for the exported vtables) and compares. Exported DATA symbols (`randombytes_{internal,sysrandom}_implementation`, `ipcrypt_soft_implementation`) are resolved via `Symbol::into_raw().into_raw()` — `Library::get::<T>` rejects non-pointer-sized `T`.\n\n## Changes under `translation/src/`\n\n1. **`src/randombytes_sysrandom.rs`** — `randombytes_linux_getrandom()`: added the C `assert(chunk_size > (size_t) 0U)`. **Real divergence found**: the reference C build compiles with assertions live (`CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` empty → no `-DNDEBUG`), so calling `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation.buf(ptr, 0)` directly (legal — the vtable is public; `randombytes_buf()` itself short-circuits size 0) aborted in C (exit 134 / SIGABRT) while Rust returned silently (exit 0). Now both give SIGABRT. Covered by `g6e_vtable_buf_zero_size` (ERRORS 664/666/675/678/680/682).\n2. **`src/randombytes_internal.rs`** — same assert added to the identical chunking wrapper in the `internal` backend (unreachable via that backend, whose `.buf` uses ChaCha20; added for faithfulness — its `.buf(ptr,0)` correctly stays quiet in both libraries, which the test also asserts).\n\nNo other Rust fixes were needed: all other comparisons (ML-KEM-768, X-Wing, all four ipcrypt variants incl. the degenerate-key branch, `randombytes_buf_deterministic`, `randombytes_uniform` rejection arithmetic) matched C on the first run. To confirm the tests are not vacuous I temporarily mutated `randombytes.rs` (`< 2` → `< 1`) and `ipcrypt.rs` (`^ 0x5a` → `^ 0x5b`): 4 t09 tests + 3 t10 tests failed; both files were then restored byte-identically (verified by `diff`).\n\n## Row coverage\n\nAll of CONFIGS 889-995 and ERRORS 639-739 are covered, each test carrying a comment naming its rows. Rows that cannot be exercised through the public API are enumerated with reasons in `g6e_unreachable_rows_documented` (kept as a test so the list shows in the run output):\n\n- **642, 710, 714, 719, 730, 731** — \"no length check\": exercising them is an out-of-bounds read (UB), not an observable error.\n- **643** — `assert(buf_len <= SIZE_MAX)` is a tautology on 64-bit (the u64→size_t forwarding *is* tested up to `u64::MAX` with a record-only `buf` hook).\n- **652, 653, 655, 656-663, 665-672, 673, 674-679, 681, 683-698, 703-706** — `static` I/O-failure branches / `assert`s of the two entropy backends: need `getrandom()` to fail, `/dev/urandom` to be unopenable, `gettimeofday` to fail, a `_WIN32`/`getentropy`/CommonCrypto build, or ChaCha20 to return non-zero. Their success sides are driven hard instead (sizes 1/255/256/257/511/512/513/4096/65536 through both backends, plus the `rnd32` pool refill).\n- **699** — fork-safety `sodium_misuse()`: `HAVE_GETPID` is not defined in this build, so no pid check is compiled in.\n- **721** — dead code (`crypto_kem_mlkem768_dec` never returns non-zero; `LCOV_EXCL`).\n- **930** — `randombytes_salsa20_implementation` is a preprocessor alias, not a symbol (asserted absent in both libraries).\n- **936, 952** — no ACVP / X-Wing published KAT vectors are available offline, so the assertion is C-vs-Rust byte equality plus a structural check of the ML-KEM sk layout (`pk ‖ SHA3-256(pk) ‖ z`) and of the X-Wing `SHAKE256(seed,96)` 64+32 split using each library's own SHA3/SHAKE/X25519.\n- **995** — only the soft ipcrypt backend is compiled in either library (no `HAVE_WMMINTRIN_H`/`HAVE_ARMCRYPTO`), so `_crypto_ipcrypt_pick_best_implementation()` is verified to return 0 and to leave the outputs unchanged.\n\nOne inherent limitation worth flagging: the `internal` and `sysrandom` backends draw real kernel entropy, so their `buf`/`random` outputs can only be compared structurally (no repeats, non-zero, no abort, identical `close()`/`stir()` return sequences). Everything deterministic (`randombytes_buf_deterministic`, `randombytes_uniform` with a sequenced custom implementation, all KEM and ipcrypt paths, and `_keypair`/`_enc` under `install_det_random()` + `det_reseed()`) is compared byte-for-byte."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1245,"cache_read_input_tokens":264954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1245,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DNV3yeoCcYcj614Es8SD1r","session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f","uuid":"8a6d6aec-b4a4-45c9-b738-d69f5e2eb59a","timestamp":"2026-09-08T15:55:50.791Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write G6 randombytes/kem/ipcrypt tests"}
