{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_onpmdfajy4uaiqinf6tqjsqfjkfsowrk3bkk326gmnhcmfsjg7za","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both suites are green. Final report:\n\n## Deliverables\n\n| File | `#[test]` fns | Final result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation/tests/t11_g2_pwhash.rs` | 22 | `test result: ok. 22 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ... finished in 6.70s` |\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-li83Jg/translation/tests/t12_g2_pwhash_errors.rs` | 21 (incl. `zz_abort_child`) | `test result: ok. 21 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ... finished in 69.67s` |\n\nEvery test goes through `pair::<F>()` (dlsym from both `.so`s) and compares return value + full output buffer (with guard bytes past the requested length) + `errno`. Both files end with a comment block naming every row that is *not* asserted and why.\n\n## Changes under `translation/src/`\n\n**Only one real bug, in the `argon2_type` FFI parameter** (found via `_sodium_argon2_ctx(ctx, 0)` returning `0` in Rust vs `ARGON2_INCORRECT_TYPE` (-26) in C).\n\nThe Rust modelled `argon2_type` as a 2-variant `#[repr(C)] enum`, so C's reachable `default:` arms were dropped and out-of-range ints were UB. Fixed in all three of my files by making it a faithful `#[repr(transparent)] pub struct argon2_type(pub c_int)` with `Argon2_i`/`Argon2_id` associated consts (ABI-identical, all existing `==`/`!=`/field uses unchanged):\n\n- `src/argon2.rs` — new type definition; `(*instance).type_ as u64` → `.0 as u64`; `type_ as u32` → `type_.0 as u32` in `argon2_initial_hash`. `_sodium_argon2_ctx`'s existing `if type_ != Argon2_id && type_ != Argon2_i { return ARGON2_INCORRECT_TYPE }` now actually fires.\n- `src/argon2_encoding.rs` — new type definition; the exhaustive `match type_` in `_sodium_argon2_decode_string` became `if/else if/else → ARGON2_INCORRECT_TYPE`, and in `_sodium_argon2_encode_string` `else → ARGON2_ENCODING_FAIL`, matching the C `default:` arms (ERRORS.md rows 202, 223, and row 153 via `argon2_ctx`).\n- `src/pwhash_argon2.rs` — new type definition (no behavioural change; it only ever passes the two valid values).\n\nNo other `src/` file needed a change — the Rust translation of argon2/scrypt was otherwise byte-exact on every valid and error path tested, including the RFC 7914 vectors and fixed argon2i/argon2id known answers.\n\n## Corrections to the Phase-A tables (test comments record each)\n\n- **CONFIGS row 220**: `memlimit = 4398046511104` is *rejected* (`/1024` → 4294967296 > `UINT32_MAX`); the largest accepted value is `4398046511103`. Both asserted.\n- **CONFIGS row 253**: scrypt `needs_rehash` with `memlimit = 1 GiB` returns **0**, not 1 — with `opslimit=32768 < memlimit/32` `pickparams` takes the `p=1` branch where `memlimit` is unused. Added `memlimit ∈ {0,1024,16384}` for a genuine `1`.\n- **ERRORS row 219**: an invalid-base64 *hash* field yields `ARGON2_OUTPUT_TOO_SHORT` (-2), not `DECODING_FAIL` — `sodium_base642bin` is called with a non-NULL `b64_end` and so succeeds with `bin_len = 0`.\n- **ERRORS row 183/221**: `p > ARGON2_MAX_LANES` gives `LANES_TOO_MANY` (lanes are validated before `m_cost`).\n- **ERRORS row 225**: `sodium_bin2base64` with a too-small destination calls `sodium_misuse()` in this build, so `argon2_encode_string` **aborts** rather than returning `ARGON2_ENCODING_FAIL`. Characterised exactly (argon2i: `0..=26` and `49` → `-31`; `27..=48` and `50..=92` → SIGABRT; `93+` → OK) and moved to the abort-child harness (`g2_err_row_225_encode_b64_misuse`).\n- **ERRORS rows 234/239**: reachable after all — `opslimit = UINT64_MAX, memlimit = 0` makes `pickparams` pick `p = 134217727`, so `B_size = 128 GiB` and the region allocation fails (`_ll` → `ENOMEM`; `_str` → `EINVAL`). Both asserted.\n- **`escrypt_parse_setting` NUL quirk**: `decode64_one` uses `strchr(itoa64, src)`, which matches the terminating NUL, so a `0x00` byte decodes to 64 instead of failing. Asserted as shared behaviour.\n- **Region allocator**: neither library imports `mmap`/`posix_memalign` for `escrypt_alloc_region`; the `malloc(size+63)` fallback is compiled, so the `munmap`-failure rows (249/281) do not exist in this build.\n\n## Rows not covered (with reasons)\n\n- **CONFIGS 176, 187** — MODERATE *memlimit* profiles (128/256 MiB); explicitly out of scope per instructions. The MODERATE/SENSITIVE *opslimit* values are covered at `MEMLIMIT_MIN`, and one INTERACTIVE spot-check per algorithm plus scrypt is included.\n- **CONFIGS 267** — `_ll` with `r*p == 2^30-1` needs 128 GiB; only its allocation-failure behaviour is observable (asserted in t12).\n- **CONFIGS 228** — a rejection row; covered in t12 (row 220, padded salt and padded hash).\n- **ERRORS 104, 115, 156, 161, 230** — the C writes/randomises `out` over the full `outlen` *before* the check, so triggering them needs a real 4 GiB / 137 GiB buffer.\n- **ERRORS 114, 124, 154, 158, 162, 191-194, 196, 197** — require an injected allocation failure. Row 195 (`NULL` args) is asserted, and rows 114/124's callee failure returns are fully covered via `_sodium_argon2{i,id}_hash_raw` (rows 152/155/157/159).\n- **ERRORS 111, 138, 143, 169, 171, 172, 175, 177, 178, 180, 181, 185, 188, 208, 211, 214, 229, 232, 235-238, 241, 244, 248, 249, 258, 267, 268, 272, 274, 279, 281** — statically dead in this configuration (MIN==0, `uint32_t` field width, `pickparams` always returns 0, pre-checked \"can't happen\" branches, non-compiled `munmap` path). For each of these the *accepted* boundary value is asserted instead (e.g. `m_cost = 0xFFFFFFFF`, `t_cost = 0xFFFFFFFF`, `threads = 0xFFFFFF`, `saltlen = 8`, `outlen = 16`)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":359,"cache_read_input_tokens":351550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":359,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AJX15YUFCoqDqVKRXn4NVP","session_id":"37babf23-64fa-4bd5-8c90-ef1a4034e89f","uuid":"01da5da8-cdc5-4bd3-bc51-1a65fa0cdca7","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:08:12.262Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write G2 pwhash differential tests"}
